This project models a dual‑port ROM system with a small pipeline stage and a simple arbitration logic, then tests it with different address patterns in the testbench.

Very high level:

There is a ROM that can be read from two addresses at the same time (port A and port B).

The ROM outputs are registered (pipelined) once.

A small arbitration block decides what to drive on out_a and out_b if both ports access the same address.

The testbench drives different address combinations to check:

both ports reading the same address

both ports reading different consecutive addresses

one port counting up, the other counting down (forward/backward)

Let’s go block by block.

1. Top module: dual_address_rom_system
This is the main DUT that connects all submodules.

Ports:

Inputs:

clk, rst

addr_a, addr_b (addresses for port A and port B)

Outputs:

out_a, out_b (final data outputs after pipeline + arbitration)

Internal signals:

From ROM: rom_data_a, rom_data_b

Pipeline outputs:

addresses: pipe_addr_a, pipe_addr_b

data: pipe_data_a, pipe_data_b

Connections:

ROM:

Inputs: clk, addr_a, addr_b

Outputs: rom_data_a, rom_data_b

Pipeline registers:

Inputs:

clk, rst

addr_a_in = addr_a

addr_b_in = addr_b

data_a_in = rom_data_a

data_b_in = rom_data_b

Outputs:

addr_a_out = pipe_addr_a

addr_b_out = pipe_addr_b

data_a_out = pipe_data_a

data_b_out = pipe_data_b

Arbitration:

Inputs:

addr_a = pipe_addr_a

addr_b = pipe_addr_b

data_a = pipe_data_a

data_b = pipe_data_b

Outputs:

out_a, out_b

So data path is:

addr_a/addr_b → ROM → pipeline_registers → arbitration → out_a/out_b (with one clock of latency between each stage).

2. Dual-port ROM: dual_port_rom
This block stores constant data and supports two simultaneous reads.

Key points:

Memory declaration:
reg [DATA_WIDTH-1:0] mem [0:(1<<ADDR_WIDTH)-1];
With ADDR_WIDTH = 6 → depth = 2^6 = 64 locations (0 to 63).

Initialization:

initial block uses $readmemh("rom_project.memh", mem); to load ROM contents from a hex file at time 0.

Then it prints first few entries for debug: mem[0], mem[1], mem[2].

Synchronous read:


verilog
always @(posedge clk) begin
    data_a <= mem[addr_a];
    data_b <= mem[addr_b];
end
This means:

At every rising edge of clk, ROM samples addr_a and addr_b.

On that same edge, outputs data_a and data_b are updated with the corresponding memory values.

So the ROM introduces 1 clock cycle latency from address to data.

3. Pipeline stage: pipeline_registers
This stage registers both addresses and data again, adding another pipeline stage.

Ports:

Inputs:

clk, rst

addr_a_in, addr_b_in

data_a_in, data_b_in

Outputs:

addr_a_out, addr_b_out

data_a_out, data_b_out

Behavior:

On reset (rst = 1):

All outputs are cleared to 0.

On each posedge clk when rst = 0:

addr_a_out <= addr_a_in;

addr_b_out <= addr_b_in;

data_a_out <= data_a_in;

data_b_out <= data_b_in;

So overall latency from external address to arbitration inputs:

1 clock inside ROM

1 clock in pipeline registers
→ 2 clock cycles latency from addr_a/addr_b to pipe_data_a/pipe_data_b.

4. Arbitration: data_arbitration
This is a simple combinational block that decides what the final outputs should be.

Inputs:

Addresses: addr_a, addr_b (these are pipeline outputs)

Data: data_a, data_b (also pipeline outputs)

Logic:


verilog
always @(*) begin
    if (addr_a == addr_b) begin
        // SAME ADDRESS ACCESS
        out_a = data_a;
        out_b = data_a;
    end
    else begin
        // DIFFERENT ADDRESS ACCESS
        out_a = data_a;
        out_b = data_b;
    end
end
Meaning:

If both ports access the same address:

Both outputs drive the same data (data_a).

If ports access different addresses:

out_a gets data_a (port A’s data).

out_b gets data_b (port B’s data).

This block is modeling a policy: in case of same-address access, both outputs are identical.

5. Testbench: tb_dual_address_rom
The testbench instantiates the top module and stimulates it with different address patterns.

5.1. Declarations and DUT
Parameters: ADDR_WIDTH = 6, DATA_WIDTH = 8

Signals:

clk, rst

addr_a, addr_b

out_a, out_b

integer i;

DUT instantiation with matching parameters.

5.2. Clock generation

verilog
always #5 clk = ~clk;
Clock period = 10 ns.

Frequency = 100 MHz (conceptually).

5.3. Initial block (test sequence)
VCD dump:
//and remove this to view on vivado 
$dumpfile("dump.vcd");

$dumpvars(0, tb_dual_address_rom);
To view waveforms in GTKWave

Reset sequence:

clk = 0;

rst = 1;

addr_a = 0;

addr_b = 0;

Wait 20 ns: #20;

rst = 0;

After this, DUT starts operating normally.

Same address access loop:


verilog
for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) begin
    @(posedge clk);
    addr_a = i;
    addr_b = i;
    @(posedge clk);
    $display("TIME=%0t ADDR_A=%0d DATA_A=%h(%c) | ADDR_B=%0d DATA_B=%h(%c)",
        $time, addr_a, out_a, out_a, addr_b, out_b, out_b);
end
Step-by-step for each iteration:

Wait for a clock edge.

Apply same address on both ports: addr_a = i; addr_b = i;

Wait one more clock edge (to let pipeline + ROM update).

Print time, addresses, and data (in hex and ASCII).
This checks that out_a and out_b are equal when both ports read same address.

Different address access loop:


verilog
for (i = 0; i < 20; i = i + 1) begin
    @(posedge clk);
    addr_a = i;
    addr_b = i + 1;
    @(posedge clk);
    $display(...);
end
Here:

Port A reads address i.

Port B reads address i+1.

Checks that outputs show data from two different addresses.

Forward / backward access loop:


verilog
for (i = 0; i < 20; i = i + 1) begin
    @(posedge clk);
    addr_a = i;
    addr_b = 63 - i;
    @(posedge clk);
    $display(...);
end
Port A counts upward from 0.

Port B counts downward from 63.

Tests “crossing” address patterns and how arbitration behaves when addresses differ and sometimes may meet in the middle.

Finish:

#20;

$finish;

So the testbench is mainly a functional stimulus to observe:

Same-address behavior (both outputs equal).

Different-address behavior.

How the 2-cycle pipeline affects timing.
